{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxfhLPgUb8vnhbuE7ao5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017npcxef7MA2Ga6odCe836J","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/collab/1792280000000-review-6b-task-transport.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15284,"cache_read_input_tokens":12374,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15284},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"195d2023-8c95-40ce-b61e-3288e37d4c05","uuid":"eb71f311-f80c-4826-864f-be71d08f67b4","timestamp":"2026-10-12T16:35:34.536Z","request_id":"req_011CfxfhL8otVyKJ79jpsYi7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017npcxef7MA2Ga6odCe836J":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/collab/1792280000000-review-6b-task-transport.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017npcxef7MA2Ga6odCe836J","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Slice 6b task-transport review\n2\t\n3\tReviewer: GPT-6.1-sol, non-author. Reviewed branch `srvm-phase6b`, HEAD\n4\t`c3d37586`, `git diff master...HEAD`, the engineer report, and continuation\n5\tdesign §§3–8. No implementation or test changes remain. Temporary mutants\n6\twere restored with `cp`, checked against the backup with `diff`, and checked\n7\twith `git diff --quiet -- src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` after every run.\n8\t\n9\tThe reference transport works on the supplied corpus, but its pre-attachment\n10\tvalidation is incomplete and its dependency discharge can claim an unavailable\n11\tbinding. These are merge blockers despite the green suites.\n12\t\n13\t## Findings\n14\t\n15\t| ID | Severity | File:line | Finding and required change |\n16\t|---|---|---|---|\n17\t| F1 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1535`, `:1608`, `:1681`, `:1726` | Carrier-derived store and closure refusals occur **after attachment**. A re-emitted row-1 carrier with store entry `yin/def → [:lit 42]` attaches once, then throws `{:rule :reserved-name :role :store-key}` from `engine/store-put`. Changing a carried closure's entry to `999999999` likewise attaches once, then throws `:origin-not-attached` from `module/lower-closure`. `check-task!` checks closure segment presence only. Preflight reserved keys in every root/module store and closure entry/binder agreement with A for the entire task tree before attachment; normalize carrier defects to the transport's named refusals. Add zero-attach tests for both cases. |\n18\t| F2 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1518`, `:1565`, `:1693` | Install records are not adequately checked before restoration. Replacing row 10's retained response with `{}` passes `decode`/`check-task!`, attaches once, then `spawn-child` rejects its nil image with `{:defect {:rule :nonempty :pc 0}}`. Setting its phase to `:bogus` is accepted and installed into the restored machine. Validate phase and the retained response/image contract, including the image needed by spawning, before attachment. Check their relationship to the carried child; restoration must not be the first interpreter to discover malformed install data. |\n19\t| F3 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1379`, `:1386` | Dependency discharge treats keys of **all** source module stores as globally available bindings. A halted `(fn [] ghost)` correctly refuses with missing obligation `ghost`. Adding `:module-stores {'unrelated {'ghost 42}}` makes the same lift and lower succeed, although the carrier has **zero** module-store rows and the closure has no store-of. Invoking the restored closure fails `Unable to resolve symbol: ghost in this context`. `engine/active-store` selects one store; `resolve-var` does not search their union. Discharge each free occurrence in its applicable environment/store context and require the answering state to travel. Add this negative case and a positive scoped module-store case. |\n20\t| F4 | Medium | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1517` | Custody is checked before pending/operations and dependencies, contrary to §§3.3/7.2 steps 8, 9, 10. A carrier with both absent tree associations and non-nil custody reports `:yin.k/unsupported :custody`, masking the earlier dependency defect. Move custody after those checks, with stage ordering across the task tree, and add a combined-defect test. |\n21\t| F5 | Medium | `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc:434`, `:462`, `:938` | The harness does not detect redundant attachment or skipped dependency discovery. The duplicate-attach and closure-before-walk mutants both pass all 357 quick assertions and all 609 matrix assertions. Assert successful attachment count equals the number of distinct stream identities, including shared identities across waiters/children. Add a carried closure whose only free obligation is missing, plus reachable/transitive dependency cases, so moving closure discovery ahead of the walk fails. |\n22\t\n23\t### Reproduction method\n24\t\n25\tThe probes used the harness's public fixture functions without editing tests:\n26\t`[toy wire] = (h/lift-bare n)`, `task = (h/root-task wire)`, edit the abstract\n27\ttask, then `(h/lowered toy (rows/emit edited-task))`. Thus row hashes and\n28\tnumbering were recomputed, rather than bypassing a hash check.\n29\t\n30\tEdits for F1/F2/F4:\n31\t\n32\t```clojure\n33\t;; F1, row 1: append to the root store's pairs\n34\t(update-in task [:slots 6 :slots] into ['yin/def [:lit 42]])\n35\t;; F1, row 1: select a :closure node and change its entry slot\n36\t(assoc-in closure-node [:slots 1] 999999999)\n37\t;; F2, row 10\n38\t(assoc-in task [:slots 12 0 :slots 3] {})\n39\t(assoc-in task [:slots 12 0 :slots 1] :bogus)\n40\t;; F4, row 1\n41\t(-> task\n42\t    (assoc-in [:slots 10 :yin.k/trees] {})\n43\t    (assoc-in [:slots 16] {:custody :exclusive}))\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tF3 used a real evaluator-produced closure from\n47\t`(vm/run (h/load-program (h/machine) (s/lam [] (s/v 'ghost))))`, with the\n48\tunrelated module-store map added before lift. After lower, storing the returned\n49\tclosure as `f` and running `(f)` reproduced the missing-name error. Probe code\n50\tand compact outcomes are in `/private/tmp/review-6b-probes.clj` and\n51\t`/private/tmp/review-6b-probes.log` for this session.\n52\t\n53\t## Implementation assessment against §7\n54\t\n55\tLift checks ready queue, holds and control before its walk; reads retained\n56\t`:free-env` with explicit override; authenticates resource references; builds\n57\tall pending variants; collects addresses from waits, K, parked states, closure\n58\tvalues, nested values and module stores; and checks absent **or empty** source\n59\ttree associations. Module-store discovery reaches stores referenced by values\n60\tin previously discovered stores. Dependency discovery happens after this walk,\n61\twhich is necessary, but its answering predicate has F3's scope error.\n62\t\n63\tLower's `decode` preserves gate → structure/graph → hash → canonical form →\n64\tnumbering → frames. It then visits the task tree before calling the attacher,\n65\tbut that visit omits the checks identified in F1/F2 and checks custody too\n66\tearly (F4). It therefore does **not** satisfy “all refusals before any attach.”\n67\tThe correct deduplication branch at lines 1548–1549 attaches once per stream\n68\tidentity across the whole tree. That property is implemented but inadequately\n69\ttested (F5).\n70\t\n71\tCode restoration attaches carried v4 vectors and maps frame/closure addresses\n72\tthrough the newly built aliases; source-local segment numbers do not travel.\n73\tClosures, reified continuations, stream refs and cursor refs are rebuilt for\n74\tthe receiver. Tail entries have through-K delivery and no env/window/pc.\n75\tInstall children are spawned and their carried state restored without\n76\t`vm/run` or a delivery; the spawn temporarily loads an image, and restore\n77\treplaces the initial control. Free env is installed as both `:env` and\n78\t`:free-env`. These successful-path properties are sound on the checked corpus.\n79\t\n80\t## Engineer deviations 1–10\n81\t\n82\t| # | Decision | Design section and wording hint |\n83\t|---|---|---|\n84\t| 1 | Accept. The source index belongs to the composition; the kernel has no such field. | §§6.1/7.1: explicitly document `:source-index {A #{tree-address}}` as a lift input and absence/empty-set refusal. No new persisted source-index slot is needed. |\n85\t| 2 | Accept. Consistency with the landed row codec is appropriate. | §§7.1/7.2: “Refusals throw `ex-info`; ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a kind when applicable, and defect data.” |\n86\t| 3 | Accept fork-only scope; object to custody's check position (F4). | §7.2/§8.4: “Phase 6 emits nil custody and refuses non-nil custody at step 10; exclusive/holder support is phase 7.” Preserve the step order. |\n87\t| 4 | Accept as a composition prerequisite, not a proof that arbitrary receivers can resolve linked names. No registry slot exists. | §§2.6/7.2: say linked registry entries do not travel, and the receiver supplies required registry bindings. Row 17 should expressly name that prerequisite. |\n88\t| 5 | Accept lift-only name discharge as the §7.1 scope, with lower checking carried dependencies and receiver primitives. Object to the discharge implementation (F3). | §7.1: require occurrence/context-sensitive discharge against state that is carried or explicitly supplied by composition. §7.2: state which receiver obligations are checked before attach and which registry prerequisites belong to composition; do not describe the current union of module-store keys as resolve-var order. |\n89\t| 6 | Accept whole root store and all parked records as the conservative slice. | §§2.6/7.1: “The phase-6b store slice is the complete root store; every parked record travels.” Keep module-store reachability separate. |\n90\t| 7 | Accept keeping the receiver's supplied registry. This companion design does not require isolated-receiver pruning. | §7.2: explicitly retain the supplied module registry, with the prerequisite from deviation 4. |\n91\t| 8 | Accept. Calling `validate-frames` before emit meets §3.3; a second decode is unnecessary. | §7.1: describe validation of the assembled abstract task, followed by emit; no design change to the invariant. |\n92\t| 9 | Accept naming previously unspecified kinds, provided defects get named transport outcomes and preserve stage order. Current late raw errors are not acceptable (F1/F2). | §7.2: add a refusal table for role, install completeness/shape/phase, task kind, parked ID, requires, segment, store keys and closure origin/binders. Custody remains step 10. |\n93\t| 10 | Accept the fixture mechanics, with explicit synthetic labeling for row 18's symbolic module-store key. Rows 13/20 follow the stated beside-resume and two-batch semantics. | §5.2 row 18: distinguish this hand-built store-key fixture from actual address-keyed installed stores; row 17 already exercises a real installation. No change needed for rows 13/20. |\n94\t\n95\t## Design bugs B1–B4\n96\t\n97\t| Bug | Decision | Exact amendment target and wording hint |\n98\t|---|---|---|\n99\t| B1 | Accept the engine-state diagnosis. `advance-install` lines 1444–1454 assigns blocked children `:parked`, and routes halted/explicitly parked children through linking. The harness's row 11 is a manually changed phase, not the advertised executable park-child scenario. | §5.2 rows 10–12 and §8.2 acceptance 3: row 10 is child kind `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 12 uses that child with tail delivery. Rewrite row 11 as an explicitly synthetic phase-`:running` transport case, or obtain an owner-defined new reachable scenario. Do not continue to promise an executable kind-`:parked` install child on the current engine. |\n100\t| B2 | Accept. The wrapper's `x` shadows the free `x` before `f` is created. | §5.2 wrapper-results paragraph and row 21: state the exception: depth-3 returns `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`; bare, operand and captured retain the corresponding 99 result. Alternatively change wrapper binders in the design and harness together; the current expected exception is correct. |\n101\t| B3 | Accept wire refusal `:row-shape`; object to the old `:frame-shape` wording. | §5.2 refusal rows: “a fourth tail slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2.” S1's abstract frame-shape rule remains valid. Separately note the pre-existing abstract emitter's extra-slot truncation; this review did not change 6a code. |\n102\t| B4 | Accept. Segment discovery depends on walking the task, including stored closure values and module stores. | §7.1: order as “preflight quiescence/holds/safepoint; classify kind; walk the task and discover segments/stores/cursor cells; discharge dependencies and check tree coverage; validate frames; canonical-number and emit.” Preserve the lower's distinct numbered-stage order. |\n103\t\n104\t## Harness and portability\n105\t\n106\tThe matrix performs 89 actual labelled runs and asserts both case count and\n107\tcompleted-run count. Each builder receives the selected wrapper and is called\n108\tseparately for reference and transport. Four wrappers construct different\n109\tprograms/K shapes; the counter is not incremented without executing `laws`.\n110\tHowever, “89 distinct scenarios” would overstate it: rows **3 and 24 use the\n111\tsame `sent-ffi` function**, so there are 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups.\n112\tThis duplication is intentional in §5.2 (row 24 is row 3's parked bookkeeping\n113\tfacet); the separate `act-ffi-bookkeeping` test checks its identity and shape.\n114\tDescribe the count as 89 labelled row/segment executions, not 89 unique states.\n115\t\n116\t`laws` asserts law 1 on completion, normalized result and effect trace; law 2\n117\ton canonical root equality; law 3 on the observed first wait's reason for the\n118\tblocked corpus; law 6 through recursive task/frame validation. The fixture's\n119\tblocked worlds have the intended single parent wait; the multiwaiter test is\n120\tseparate. Law 6 also traverses parked/captured states and install children.\n121\t\n122\tLaw 2 is not vacuous: source correspondence comes from served-table identities,\n123\tand relift correspondence comes from the attacher's recorded reflection-handle\n124\t→ source-identity relation. It is not inferred from the two carriers being\n125\tcompared or their traversal position. `canonical-under` rewrites stream\n126\tidentity/descriptor and re-emits, retaining code, values, windows, pending data\n127\tand cursor alias topology. Its coverage check rejects absent correspondence\n128\tkeys. The fixture could assert non-nil recorded origins more explicitly, but\n129\tits known attached reflections have recorded origins. Law 2's inability to\n130\tobserve redundant attacher invocations explains M6, not a tautological root\n131\toracle.\n132\t\n133\tPortability read: no new `for` comprehension over long sequences, private-var\n134\taccess, unary-minus call, or multi-key `assoc` with a nil initial target was\n135\tfound. Restore targets are maps/records; optional maps are handled through\n136\t`cond->` or `merge`. Catches use the correct reader-conditional throwable type.\n137\tDart `fn?` accepts Dart `Function` or the `Fn` marker (pinned local\n138\t`cljd/core.cljd:468`); `dao.jing.cbor/numeric?` handles the portable numeric\n139\tcarriers, including its Dart branches. Their new uses are consistent with\n140\tthose implementations, not merely justified by precedent in handoff.\n141\tNode/Dart execution remains unverified, as requested by the review brief.\n142\t\n143\tThe alleged `handoff.cljc` latent symbol bug is **not a bug**. Its actual\n144\tlines 1483–1484 use `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name x)))`. JVM evaluation\n145\tproduced `host.mod/f`, namespace `\"host.mod\"`, equality with `'host.mod/f`\n146\ttrue. The pinned Dart `symbol` implementation (`cljd/core.cljd:1970`) also\n147\tsplits the one-string argument at `/`; ClojureScript's symbol has the same\n148\tqualified-symbol contract. The new two-argument form is fine, but the\n149\tengineer's claim that the old form has a nil namespace should be withdrawn.\n150\tNo handoff edit was made.\n151\t\n152\tThe store-write-audit allowlist additions are structurally justified: the\n153\troot store is rebuilt through `engine/store-put` and assigned to the fresh\n154\treceiver once, with no new program-write bypass. The comment correctly\n155\tidentifies reserved-key refusal, but this does not prove its timing. F1 shows\n156\tthat the refusal currently happens too late. Module-store restoration also\n157\tneeds the preflight key validation described in F1.\n158\t\n159\t## Independent mutation results\n160\t\n161\tAll nine mutants were run against the quick harness. The two survivors were\n162\talso run against the slow matrix. Mutations were sequential, foreground, and\n163\trestored with `cp` even when a test failed. No audit allowlist was changed to\n164\taccommodate mutants.\n165\t\n166\t| Mutant | Concrete change | Result |\n167\t|---|---|---|\n168\t| M1 lower attaches before the last refusal | Obtain handles before `check-task!`, preserving decode first. | Killed: missing-tree refusal observes one attach (`refusals:807`). |\n169\t| M2 lift serves before safepoint refusal | Serve the first available resource inside the control-refusal branch. | Killed: safepoint refusal observes one serve (`refusals:860`). |\n170\t| M3 free-env override ignored | Use machine free-env in the explicit-override branch. | Killed: `free-env-shadowing` errors with inconsistent state; quick total 354 assertions, 1 error. |\n171\t| M4 tail retains a window | Add `:window {}` to restored tail payload. | Killed: three failures in `tail-states-lower-to-through-k-delivery`. |\n172\t| M5 pending ready queue need not be empty | Disable the initial ready-queue refusal. | Killed: `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`, 1 failure. |\n173\t| M6 duplicate attach for one identity | Invoke `remote/lower-marker` again in the already-present-identity branch. | **Survives quick and slow**: 357 + 609 assertions, no failures/errors. |\n174\t| M7 install child not restored | Keep the newly spawned initial child instead of calling recursive restore. | Killed: 9 failures/2 errors, including non-nil child control, missing wait and blocked status. |\n175\t| M8 values not re-sealed | Return a raw reified-continuation payload instead of `values/continuation`. | Killed: 4 failures/2 errors, including capture/round-trip failures. This mutant targets the continuation branch; it does not establish separate owner-change coverage for every value kind. |\n176\t| M9 closure computed before walk | Run discharge against the accumulator's still-empty segment set before the walk; remove the later discharge. | **Survives quick and slow**: 357 + 609 assertions, no failures/errors. |\n177\t\n178\tMutation logs are `/private/tmp/review-6b-M*.log` and\n179\t`/private/tmp/review-6b-slow-M*.log`. Backup:\n180\t`/private/tmp/review-6b-rows.backup`. Final restored source matches both the\n181\tbackup and HEAD; the saved pre-mutation working diff was empty.\n182\t\n183\t## Verification\n184\t\n185\t- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **45 tests, 939 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n186\t- Same command with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 617 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**. The matrix alone contributes 609 assertions; the other slow test is the codec counterexample search.\n187\t- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the three changed files: **0 errors, 0 warnings**.\n188\t- Neither Node nor Dart was run; §8.2 acceptance 9's three-host gate is not established by this review.\n189\t- No commits, merges or implementation fixes were made. Only this findings file is a permanent review addition.\n190\t\n191\tVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n192\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"195d2023-8c95-40ce-b61e-3288e37d4c05","uuid":"2171440f-c53e-48bf-9fe4-442bfa60c2b1","timestamp":"2026-10-12T16:35:34.555Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/collab/1792280000000-review-6b-task-transport.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md","content":"# Slice 6b task-transport review\n\nReviewer: GPT-6.1-sol, non-author. Reviewed branch `srvm-phase6b`, HEAD\n`c3d37586`, `git diff master...HEAD`, the engineer report, and continuation\ndesign §§3–8. No implementation or test changes remain. Temporary mutants\nwere restored with `cp`, checked against the backup with `diff`, and checked\nwith `git diff --quiet -- src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` after every run.\n\nThe reference transport works on the supplied corpus, but its pre-attachment\nvalidation is incomplete and its dependency discharge can claim an unavailable\nbinding. These are merge blockers despite the green suites.\n\n## Findings\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Finding and required change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1535`, `:1608`, `:1681`, `:1726` | Carrier-derived store and closure refusals occur **after attachment**. A re-emitted row-1 carrier with store entry `yin/def → [:lit 42]` attaches once, then throws `{:rule :reserved-name :role :store-key}` from `engine/store-put`. Changing a carried closure's entry to `999999999` likewise attaches once, then throws `:origin-not-attached` from `module/lower-closure`. `check-task!` checks closure segment presence only. Preflight reserved keys in every root/module store and closure entry/binder agreement with A for the entire task tree before attachment; normalize carrier defects to the transport's named refusals. Add zero-attach tests for both cases. |\n| F2 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1518`, `:1565`, `:1693` | Install records are not adequately checked before restoration. Replacing row 10's retained response with `{}` passes `decode`/`check-task!`, attaches once, then `spawn-child` rejects its nil image with `{:defect {:rule :nonempty :pc 0}}`. Setting its phase to `:bogus` is accepted and installed into the restored machine. Validate phase and the retained response/image contract, including the image needed by spawning, before attachment. Check their relationship to the carried child; restoration must not be the first interpreter to discover malformed install data. |\n| F3 | High | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1379`, `:1386` | Dependency discharge treats keys of **all** source module stores as globally available bindings. A halted `(fn [] ghost)` correctly refuses with missing obligation `ghost`. Adding `:module-stores {'unrelated {'ghost 42}}` makes the same lift and lower succeed, although the carrier has **zero** module-store rows and the closure has no store-of. Invoking the restored closure fails `Unable to resolve symbol: ghost in this context`. `engine/active-store` selects one store; `resolve-var` does not search their union. Discharge each free occurrence in its applicable environment/store context and require the answering state to travel. Add this negative case and a positive scoped module-store case. |\n| F4 | Medium | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1517` | Custody is checked before pending/operations and dependencies, contrary to §§3.3/7.2 steps 8, 9, 10. A carrier with both absent tree associations and non-nil custody reports `:yin.k/unsupported :custody`, masking the earlier dependency defect. Move custody after those checks, with stage ordering across the task tree, and add a combined-defect test. |\n| F5 | Medium | `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc:434`, `:462`, `:938` | The harness does not detect redundant attachment or skipped dependency discovery. The duplicate-attach and closure-before-walk mutants both pass all 357 quick assertions and all 609 matrix assertions. Assert successful attachment count equals the number of distinct stream identities, including shared identities across waiters/children. Add a carried closure whose only free obligation is missing, plus reachable/transitive dependency cases, so moving closure discovery ahead of the walk fails. |\n\n### Reproduction method\n\nThe probes used the harness's public fixture functions without editing tests:\n`[toy wire] = (h/lift-bare n)`, `task = (h/root-task wire)`, edit the abstract\ntask, then `(h/lowered toy (rows/emit edited-task))`. Thus row hashes and\nnumbering were recomputed, rather than bypassing a hash check.\n\nEdits for F1/F2/F4:\n\n```clojure\n;; F1, row 1: append to the root store's pairs\n(update-in task [:slots 6 :slots] into ['yin/def [:lit 42]])\n;; F1, row 1: select a :closure node and change its entry slot\n(assoc-in closure-node [:slots 1] 999999999)\n;; F2, row 10\n(assoc-in task [:slots 12 0 :slots 3] {})\n(assoc-in task [:slots 12 0 :slots 1] :bogus)\n;; F4, row 1\n(-> task\n    (assoc-in [:slots 10 :yin.k/trees] {})\n    (assoc-in [:slots 16] {:custody :exclusive}))\n```\n\nF3 used a real evaluator-produced closure from\n`(vm/run (h/load-program (h/machine) (s/lam [] (s/v 'ghost))))`, with the\nunrelated module-store map added before lift. After lower, storing the returned\nclosure as `f` and running `(f)` reproduced the missing-name error. Probe code\nand compact outcomes are in `/private/tmp/review-6b-probes.clj` and\n`/private/tmp/review-6b-probes.log` for this session.\n\n## Implementation assessment against §7\n\nLift checks ready queue, holds and control before its walk; reads retained\n`:free-env` with explicit override; authenticates resource references; builds\nall pending variants; collects addresses from waits, K, parked states, closure\nvalues, nested values and module stores; and checks absent **or empty** source\ntree associations. Module-store discovery reaches stores referenced by values\nin previously discovered stores. Dependency discovery happens after this walk,\nwhich is necessary, but its answering predicate has F3's scope error.\n\nLower's `decode` preserves gate → structure/graph → hash → canonical form →\nnumbering → frames. It then visits the task tree before calling the attacher,\nbut that visit omits the checks identified in F1/F2 and checks custody too\nearly (F4). It therefore does **not** satisfy “all refusals before any attach.”\nThe correct deduplication branch at lines 1548–1549 attaches once per stream\nidentity across the whole tree. That property is implemented but inadequately\ntested (F5).\n\nCode restoration attaches carried v4 vectors and maps frame/closure addresses\nthrough the newly built aliases; source-local segment numbers do not travel.\nClosures, reified continuations, stream refs and cursor refs are rebuilt for\nthe receiver. Tail entries have through-K delivery and no env/window/pc.\nInstall children are spawned and their carried state restored without\n`vm/run` or a delivery; the spawn temporarily loads an image, and restore\nreplaces the initial control. Free env is installed as both `:env` and\n`:free-env`. These successful-path properties are sound on the checked corpus.\n\n## Engineer deviations 1–10\n\n| # | Decision | Design section and wording hint |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Accept. The source index belongs to the composition; the kernel has no such field. | §§6.1/7.1: explicitly document `:source-index {A #{tree-address}}` as a lift input and absence/empty-set refusal. No new persisted source-index slot is needed. |\n| 2 | Accept. Consistency with the landed row codec is appropriate. | §§7.1/7.2: “Refusals throw `ex-info`; ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a kind when applicable, and defect data.” |\n| 3 | Accept fork-only scope; object to custody's check position (F4). | §7.2/§8.4: “Phase 6 emits nil custody and refuses non-nil custody at step 10; exclusive/holder support is phase 7.” Preserve the step order. |\n| 4 | Accept as a composition prerequisite, not a proof that arbitrary receivers can resolve linked names. No registry slot exists. | §§2.6/7.2: say linked registry entries do not travel, and the receiver supplies required registry bindings. Row 17 should expressly name that prerequisite. |\n| 5 | Accept lift-only name discharge as the §7.1 scope, with lower checking carried dependencies and receiver primitives. Object to the discharge implementation (F3). | §7.1: require occurrence/context-sensitive discharge against state that is carried or explicitly supplied by composition. §7.2: state which receiver obligations are checked before attach and which registry prerequisites belong to composition; do not describe the current union of module-store keys as resolve-var order. |\n| 6 | Accept whole root store and all parked records as the conservative slice. | §§2.6/7.1: “The phase-6b store slice is the complete root store; every parked record travels.” Keep module-store reachability separate. |\n| 7 | Accept keeping the receiver's supplied registry. This companion design does not require isolated-receiver pruning. | §7.2: explicitly retain the supplied module registry, with the prerequisite from deviation 4. |\n| 8 | Accept. Calling `validate-frames` before emit meets §3.3; a second decode is unnecessary. | §7.1: describe validation of the assembled abstract task, followed by emit; no design change to the invariant. |\n| 9 | Accept naming previously unspecified kinds, provided defects get named transport outcomes and preserve stage order. Current late raw errors are not acceptable (F1/F2). | §7.2: add a refusal table for role, install completeness/shape/phase, task kind, parked ID, requires, segment, store keys and closure origin/binders. Custody remains step 10. |\n| 10 | Accept the fixture mechanics, with explicit synthetic labeling for row 18's symbolic module-store key. Rows 13/20 follow the stated beside-resume and two-batch semantics. | §5.2 row 18: distinguish this hand-built store-key fixture from actual address-keyed installed stores; row 17 already exercises a real installation. No change needed for rows 13/20. |\n\n## Design bugs B1–B4\n\n| Bug | Decision | Exact amendment target and wording hint |\n|---|---|---|\n| B1 | Accept the engine-state diagnosis. `advance-install` lines 1444–1454 assigns blocked children `:parked`, and routes halted/explicitly parked children through linking. The harness's row 11 is a manually changed phase, not the advertised executable park-child scenario. | §5.2 rows 10–12 and §8.2 acceptance 3: row 10 is child kind `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 12 uses that child with tail delivery. Rewrite row 11 as an explicitly synthetic phase-`:running` transport case, or obtain an owner-defined new reachable scenario. Do not continue to promise an executable kind-`:parked` install child on the current engine. |\n| B2 | Accept. The wrapper's `x` shadows the free `x` before `f` is created. | §5.2 wrapper-results paragraph and row 21: state the exception: depth-3 returns `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`; bare, operand and captured retain the corresponding 99 result. Alternatively change wrapper binders in the design and harness together; the current expected exception is correct. |\n| B3 | Accept wire refusal `:row-shape`; object to the old `:frame-shape` wording. | §5.2 refusal rows: “a fourth tail slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2.” S1's abstract frame-shape rule remains valid. Separately note the pre-existing abstract emitter's extra-slot truncation; this review did not change 6a code. |\n| B4 | Accept. Segment discovery depends on walking the task, including stored closure values and module stores. | §7.1: order as “preflight quiescence/holds/safepoint; classify kind; walk the task and discover segments/stores/cursor cells; discharge dependencies and check tree coverage; validate frames; canonical-number and emit.” Preserve the lower's distinct numbered-stage order. |\n\n## Harness and portability\n\nThe matrix performs 89 actual labelled runs and asserts both case count and\ncompleted-run count. Each builder receives the selected wrapper and is called\nseparately for reference and transport. Four wrappers construct different\nprograms/K shapes; the counter is not incremented without executing `laws`.\nHowever, “89 distinct scenarios” would overstate it: rows **3 and 24 use the\nsame `sent-ffi` function**, so there are 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups.\nThis duplication is intentional in §5.2 (row 24 is row 3's parked bookkeeping\nfacet); the separate `act-ffi-bookkeeping` test checks its identity and shape.\nDescribe the count as 89 labelled row/segment executions, not 89 unique states.\n\n`laws` asserts law 1 on completion, normalized result and effect trace; law 2\non canonical root equality; law 3 on the observed first wait's reason for the\nblocked corpus; law 6 through recursive task/frame validation. The fixture's\nblocked worlds have the intended single parent wait; the multiwaiter test is\nseparate. Law 6 also traverses parked/captured states and install children.\n\nLaw 2 is not vacuous: source correspondence comes from served-table identities,\nand relift correspondence comes from the attacher's recorded reflection-handle\n→ source-identity relation. It is not inferred from the two carriers being\ncompared or their traversal position. `canonical-under` rewrites stream\nidentity/descriptor and re-emits, retaining code, values, windows, pending data\nand cursor alias topology. Its coverage check rejects absent correspondence\nkeys. The fixture could assert non-nil recorded origins more explicitly, but\nits known attached reflections have recorded origins. Law 2's inability to\nobserve redundant attacher invocations explains M6, not a tautological root\noracle.\n\nPortability read: no new `for` comprehension over long sequences, private-var\naccess, unary-minus call, or multi-key `assoc` with a nil initial target was\nfound. Restore targets are maps/records; optional maps are handled through\n`cond->` or `merge`. Catches use the correct reader-conditional throwable type.\nDart `fn?` accepts Dart `Function` or the `Fn` marker (pinned local\n`cljd/core.cljd:468`); `dao.jing.cbor/numeric?` handles the portable numeric\ncarriers, including its Dart branches. Their new uses are consistent with\nthose implementations, not merely justified by precedent in handoff.\nNode/Dart execution remains unverified, as requested by the review brief.\n\nThe alleged `handoff.cljc` latent symbol bug is **not a bug**. Its actual\nlines 1483–1484 use `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name x)))`. JVM evaluation\nproduced `host.mod/f`, namespace `\"host.mod\"`, equality with `'host.mod/f`\ntrue. The pinned Dart `symbol` implementation (`cljd/core.cljd:1970`) also\nsplits the one-string argument at `/`; ClojureScript's symbol has the same\nqualified-symbol contract. The new two-argument form is fine, but the\nengineer's claim that the old form has a nil namespace should be withdrawn.\nNo handoff edit was made.\n\nThe store-write-audit allowlist additions are structurally justified: the\nroot store is rebuilt through `engine/store-put` and assigned to the fresh\nreceiver once, with no new program-write bypass. The comment correctly\nidentifies reserved-key refusal, but this does not prove its timing. F1 shows\nthat the refusal currently happens too late. Module-store restoration also\nneeds the preflight key validation described in F1.\n\n## Independent mutation results\n\nAll nine mutants were run against the quick harness. The two survivors were\nalso run against the slow matrix. Mutations were sequential, foreground, and\nrestored with `cp` even when a test failed. No audit allowlist was changed to\naccommodate mutants.\n\n| Mutant | Concrete change | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 lower attaches before the last refusal | Obtain handles before `check-task!`, preserving decode first. | Killed: missing-tree refusal observes one attach (`refusals:807`). |\n| M2 lift serves before safepoint refusal | Serve the first available resource inside the control-refusal branch. | Killed: safepoint refusal observes one serve (`refusals:860`). |\n| M3 free-env override ignored | Use machine free-env in the explicit-override branch. | Killed: `free-env-shadowing` errors with inconsistent state; quick total 354 assertions, 1 error. |\n| M4 tail retains a window | Add `:window {}` to restored tail payload. | Killed: three failures in `tail-states-lower-to-through-k-delivery`. |\n| M5 pending ready queue need not be empty | Disable the initial ready-queue refusal. | Killed: `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`, 1 failure. |\n| M6 duplicate attach for one identity | Invoke `remote/lower-marker` again in the already-present-identity branch. | **Survives quick and slow**: 357 + 609 assertions, no failures/errors. |\n| M7 install child not restored | Keep the newly spawned initial child instead of calling recursive restore. | Killed: 9 failures/2 errors, including non-nil child control, missing wait and blocked status. |\n| M8 values not re-sealed | Return a raw reified-continuation payload instead of `values/continuation`. | Killed: 4 failures/2 errors, including capture/round-trip failures. This mutant targets the continuation branch; it does not establish separate owner-change coverage for every value kind. |\n| M9 closure computed before walk | Run discharge against the accumulator's still-empty segment set before the walk; remove the later discharge. | **Survives quick and slow**: 357 + 609 assertions, no failures/errors. |\n\nMutation logs are `/private/tmp/review-6b-M*.log` and\n`/private/tmp/review-6b-slow-M*.log`. Backup:\n`/private/tmp/review-6b-rows.backup`. Final restored source matches both the\nbackup and HEAD; the saved pre-mutation working diff was empty.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **45 tests, 939 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Same command with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 617 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**. The matrix alone contributes 609 assertions; the other slow test is the codec counterexample search.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the three changed files: **0 errors, 0 warnings**.\n- Neither Node nor Dart was run; §8.2 acceptance 9's three-host gate is not established by this review.\n- No commits, merges or implementation fixes were made. Only this findings file is a permanent review addition.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n","numLines":192,"startLine":1,"totalLines":192}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017npcxef7MA2Ga6odCe836J","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
