{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0132fhfb2YGMt1EZB6GAoG7n","type":"tool_result","content":"95\t   `:yin.k/params`, a positional kernel's `:yin.k/arity`) are not those of\n96\t   the attached lambda it names: a lower mints an applicable closure, so\n97\t   the wire never chooses what a closure binds.\"\n98\t  [marker]\n99\t  (fail \"Closure marker disagrees with its attached lambda\"\n100\t        {:reason :marker-mismatch, :segment (:yin.k/segment marker)}))\n101\t\n102\t\n103\t(defn continuation-argument\n104\t  \"The one value a captured continuation is applied to. Applying a\n105\t   continuation is abortive: the current continuation is discarded and\n106\t   the argument becomes the value of the capture point. Every VM refuses\n107\t   any other arity with this same message.\"\n108\t  [args]\n109\t  (when-not (= 1 (count args))\n110\t    (throw (ex-info \"Continuation expects exactly one argument\"\n111\t                    {:reason :continuation-arity, :argc (count args)})))\n112\t  (first args))\n113\t\n114\t\n115\t(defn resolve-var\n116\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n117\t\n118\t   A reserved name (Rule R: `yin/def` is syntax, never a name) is refused\n119\t   before env or store is consulted, so no binding, store entry, or\n120\t   registry can give it a meaning. Every executing variable lookup of\n121\t   every engine goes through here.\n122\t\n123\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n124\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n125\t  (when (vm/reserved-name? name)\n126\t    (vm/refuse-reserved! :variable name))\n127\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n128\t    (val pair)\n129\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n130\t      (val pair)\n131\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n132\t        (vm/primitive-function (val pair))\n133\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n134\t                            (module/resolve-module\n135\t                              registry\n136\t                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n137\t                                           \".\"\n138\t                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n139\t          resolved\n140\t          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n141\t                {:symbol name}))))))\n142\t\n143\t\n144\t(defn check-store-key!\n145\t  \"Refuse a program store access whose key is a reserved name (Rule R):\n146\t   the definition operator is never a store key.\"\n147\t  [key]\n148\t  (when (vm/reserved-name? key)\n149\t    (vm/refuse-reserved! :store-key key)))\n150\t\n151\t\n152\t(defn store-put\n153\t  \"The one program store write: every definition transition, the\n154\t   `:vm/store-put` effect, and the four direct store instructions write\n155\t   through here, and a reserved key is refused. Every other store write\n156\t   `yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` detects is state construction on its\n157\t   allowlist; that namespace states what it detects and the residual it\n158\t   leaves to review.\"\n159\t  [store key val]\n160\t  (check-store-key! key)\n161\t  (assoc store key val))\n162\t\n163\t\n164\t;; =============================================================================\n165\t;; Module stores (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6 and r7)\n166\t;; =============================================================================\n167\t;;\n168\t;; A linked module's closures carry `:yin.k/store-of m`, the manifest\n169\t;; address of the module whose store their body resolves against. The\n170\t;; positional kernels hold the running body's `:store-of` as a register,\n171\t;; saved in every return frame and parked payload; the named kernels carry\n172\t;; it in the lexical environment under the same key, so a closure captures\n173\t;; it with its environment, a frame's saved environment restores it, and a\n174\t;; halt strips it. The receiving task instantiates each module's store\n175\t;; under `:module-stores {m {sym value}}`, never into its own `:store`.\n176\t\n177\t(def store-of-key\n178\t  \"The key a closure (and, for the named kernels, an environment) carries\n179\t   the manifest address of its module store under.\"\n180\t  :yin.k/store-of)\n181\t\n182\t\n183\t(defn env-store-of\n184\t  \"The module store a named kernel's environment `env` runs against, or\n185\t   nil at top level.\"\n186\t  [env]\n187\t  (get env store-of-key))\n188\t\n189\t\n190\t(defn without-store-of\n191\t  \"`env` with no module store context: what a halt leaves, so the next\n192\t   top-level input never routes through a stale module store.\"\n193\t  [env]\n194\t  (if (contains? env store-of-key) (dissoc env store-of-key) env))\n195\t\n196\t\n197\t(defn store-context\n198\t  \"The module store the running body of `state` routes to, or nil: the\n199\t   positional kernels' `:store-of` register, else the named kernels'\n200\t   environment.\"\n201\t  [state]\n202\t  (or (:store-of state) (env-store-of (:env state))))\n203\t\n204\t\n205\t(defn active-store\n206\t  \"The store free reads and `:store-get` resolve against: while a module\n207\t   closure runs (`store-of` non-nil), its module store, with no ambient\n208\t   fallback -- a key the module store lacks is absent; otherwise the\n209\t   task's own store.\"\n210\t  [state store-of]\n211\t  (if (some? store-of)\n212\t    (get (:module-stores state) store-of {})\n213\t    (:store state)))\n214\t\n215\t\n216\t(defn put-active\n217\t  \"Write `key` in the active store of `state`: the module store\n218\t   `store-of` names, or the task's own store at top level. Both go\n219\t   through `store-put`, so a reserved key is refused either way.\"\n220\t  [state store-of key val]\n221\t  (if (some? store-of)\n222\t    (update-in state [:module-stores store-of]\n223\t               (fn [module-store] (store-put (or module-store {}) key val)))\n224\t    (assoc state :store (store-put (