{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019VujYt8tCKQHf6My9PpBHV","type":"tool_result","content":"448\t### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n449\t\n450\t**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\n451\treview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\n452\thashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\n453\tpending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\n454\tcomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\n455\tcontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n456\t`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\n457\tjudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\n458\tis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\n459\tfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n460\t(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\n461\tand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\n462\tover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\n463\tevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n464\t`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\n465\treconstructs.\n466\t\n467\t---\n468\t\n469\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"195d2023-8c95-40ce-b61e-3288e37d4c05","uuid":"1f710829-f17b-4078-9fab-6589f5c247c5","timestamp":"2026-10-12T16:36:04.173Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n\n**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\nreview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\nhashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\npending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\ncomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\ncontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\njudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\nis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\nfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\nand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\nover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\nevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\nreconstructs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame","numLines":22,"startLine":448,"totalLines":1762}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019VujYt8tCKQHf6My9PpBHV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
